{
  "reconstructed_theorem_text": "For every finite-dimensional complete real inner-product space E with its Borel measurable structure (dimension zero is included), every twice continuously Frechet differentiable real function rho on E, and every nonnegative real constant L, suppose rho(0)=0, grad rho(0)=0, and, for every u,v in E, 0 <= D^2 rho(u)[v,v] <= L*||v||^2. Here grad is the real Hilbert Riesz representative of the derivative. Let gamma be the centered standard Gaussian on E with covariance identity; let volume be the canonical Haar/Lebesgue measure. Define Z = integral_E exp(-rho(u)) d gamma(u), q(u)=exp(-rho(u))/Z, f(u)=exp(-rho(u)/2)/sqrt(Z), and r=gamma.tilted(-rho), where a tilt normalizes the exponential weight when integrable and otherwise returns the zero measure. Then all the following hold simultaneously:\n(1) 0 < Z <= 1.\n(2) r equals volume.tilted(u |-> -(||u||^2/2 + rho(u))), and also equals gamma.withDensity(u |-> ofReal(q(u))).\n(3) r is a probability measure.\n(4) At every u in E, q(u)>0 and f(u)^2=q(u); integral_E q d gamma = 1. These are the displayed everywhere representatives.\n(5) f is twice continuously differentiable; f and grad f belong to L^2(gamma); grad rho belongs to L^2(r).\n(6) u |-> q(u)*log(q(u)) is genuinely integrable with respect to gamma.\n(7) At every u, grad f(u)=-(f(u)/2)*grad rho(u), and grad(log q)(u)=-grad rho(u), where multiplication of a vector means real scalar multiplication.\n(8) integral_E ||grad f(u)||^2 d gamma(u) = (1/4)*integral_E ||grad rho(u)||^2 d r(u).\nAll probability, density normalization, integrability, regularity of f, and gradient identities are conclusions, not additional assumptions. The L=0 case is included. The energies are unscaled squared Euclidean-gradient integrals and the coefficient is exactly 1/4. The assertion places no positive-dimension restriction on E and no positivity restriction on L beyond L>=0.",
